<!-- **The Kotlin half of SnippetVeil, loaded only where the Kotlin plugin is.**

     `plugin.xml` declares `org.jetbrains.kotlin` as an optional dependency naming this file, so
     everything registered here is present exactly when that plugin is installed and enabled, and
     absent otherwise. The plugin itself loads either way and anonymizes Java either way.

     **This file is the isolation boundary, and the boundary is a JVM one.** A class links when it is
     loaded, and a class whose constant pool names `org.jetbrains.kotlin.*` fails to link if those
     types are not on the classloader. So the rule is not "keep Kotlin code tidy" — it is that no
     class reachable from the *main* descriptor may mention a Kotlin-plugin type, or SnippetVeil
     stops loading in an IDE with Kotlin disabled. `ShippedCodeArchitectureTest` asserts that rule
     over shipped bytecode, and demonstrates it red against code written to break it.

     Deliberately not here: the **source-file gate**. It has to answer *would this file be anonymized
     on a correctly-configured IDE?* in the one configuration where no Kotlin class can be touched,
     which is why it is a plain file-extension test in the main descriptor. A gate registered here
     would be absent in exactly the case it exists for, and `.kt` would fall into silent absence —
     the false reassurance the stated refusal exists to prevent. -->
<idea-plugin>
    <!-- **The line that turns `.kt` from a stated refusal into an offer**, and the only thing in
         this plugin that a user's IDE has to be configured a particular way to have.

         **Presence is the availability signal.** The gate asks whether a support is registered *for
         `kt`* — an attribute in this file, read without instantiating the class it names — so this
         registration is what makes the third outcome unreachable on an IDE where the Kotlin plugin
         is running and this plugin's K2 declaration was accepted, and what leaves it exactly as
         reachable on one where it is not. The refusal did not stop being real when this line
         landed; it moved to the configurations where it is true, which are an IDE with the Kotlin
         plugin switched off and one in K1 mode, where the platform skips this file on the strength
         of `supportsK1="false"` in the main descriptor.

         The class named here is the only class in this plugin whose absence is a configuration
         rather than a defect. It links against `org.jetbrains.kotlin.*`, which is why it is
         registered from here rather than from `plugin.xml`, and why the gate reads this attribute
         instead of asking the class. -->
    <extensions defaultExtensionNs="com.snippetveil">
        <languageSupport extension="kt" implementationClass="com.snippetveil.plugin.kotlin.KotlinSupport"/>
    </extensions>
</idea-plugin>
